WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.448 --> 00:00:20.252
Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History, where we sit down with some of the most famous

2
00:00:20.312 --> 00:00:21.973
people from the past.

3
00:00:22.853 --> 00:00:34.264
Today's guest was an artist, sculptor, engineer, scientist, and inventor—in other words, a true Renaissance man—and one who

4
00:00:34.464 --> 00:00:37.425
actually lived during the Renaissance.

5
00:00:38.425 --> 00:01:01.271
Please help me welcome Leonardo da Thank you, Faye.

6
00:01:01.912 --> 00:01:03.032
It's lovely to be here.

7
00:01:04.168 --> 00:01:09.632
I have so many things to ask you, Leonardo, that I don't know where to begin.

8
00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:12.774
I guess we should start at the beginning!

9
00:01:12.794 --> 00:01:15.216
A genius idea, Faye.

10
00:01:16.958 --> 00:01:22.982
I was born in 1452 and grew up in a small rural town near Florence, Italy.

11
00:01:24.503 --> 00:01:29.867
My parents weren't married, and I lived most of my childhood in my wealthy father's home with my

12
00:01:29.927 --> 00:01:31.268
grandparents and uncle.

13
00:01:32.348 --> 00:01:34.930
I spent a lot of time with my mother's family too.

14
00:01:36.031 --> 00:01:42.615
My childhood was wonderful; I roamed around the countryside, studying plants and animals.

15
00:01:43.956 --> 00:01:45.057
Did you go to school?

16
00:01:46.078 --> 00:01:46.658
I did not.

17
00:01:47.238 --> 00:01:51.621
I received some instruction in reading, writing, and a little basic math.

18
00:01:52.862 --> 00:01:56.605
Everything else I taught myself or learned from experience.

19
00:01:57.746 --> 00:02:04.154
In those days children whose parents weren't married didn't have the same educational opportunities as others.

20
00:02:05.414 --> 00:02:10.137
It worked out well for me, though, since it gave me a chance to pursue my own interests.

21
00:02:11.278 --> 00:02:15.900
I was a very curious child, and the world outside was my school!

22
00:02:16.981 --> 00:02:20.043
One time I happened upon a deep, dark cave.

23
00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:27.047
I stood there astonished, torn between fear and curiosity about the things that might be inside.

24
00:02:28.207 --> 00:02:29.776
Which won?

25
00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.418
Fear or curiosity?

26
00:02:32.559 --> 00:02:33.900
Curiosity, of course!

27
00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:37.763
With me curiosity always won.

28
00:02:38.524 --> 00:02:43.028
And it paid off, for inside I found the fossil of a whale!

29
00:02:44.269 --> 00:02:47.412
Alas, I didn't stay in the countryside forever.

30
00:02:48.393 --> 00:02:53.877
I had already shown some interest in art, so when I was 14, my father took me to

31
00:02:53.957 --> 00:02:56.800
Florence to apprentice in an artisan's workshop.

32
00:02:58.076 --> 00:03:04.621
There I learned to paint, sculpt, and work with metals and leather from the master artisan Andrea del

33
00:03:04.661 --> 00:03:08.544
Verrocchio. His workshop was a lively place.

34
00:03:09.885 --> 00:03:15.809
Eventually I established a workshop to work on my own projects and train other young artists.

35
00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:20.793
This seems like a good time to talk about your art.

36
00:03:21.933 --> 00:03:30.701
Your portrait of Lisa del Giocondo, famously known as the Mona Lisa, is probably the most famous painting

37
00:03:30.881 --> 00:03:31.982
in the world!

38
00:03:33.063 --> 00:03:33.624
Oh, that?

39
00:03:34.284 --> 00:03:35.345
I never finished it!

40
00:03:36.326 --> 00:03:42.472
I kept tinkering with it and never gave it to the person who commissioned it, Francesco del Giocondo,

41
00:03:43.033 --> 00:03:43.954
Lisa's husband.

42
00:03:45.075 --> 00:03:50.279
Finally, I had some trouble with my hand and had to stop working on it before it was

43
00:03:50.339 --> 00:03:57.373
done. I don't know what you could do to improve the Mona Lisa— it's already a masterpiece!

44
00:03:57.433 --> 00:03:59.815
And it looks so realistic!

45
00:04:00.836 --> 00:04:06.460
In the painting I used a technique called sfumato, which is the Italian word for smoke.

46
00:04:07.801 --> 00:04:12.325
Using oil paints, I gently blended the colors to blur the lines.

47
00:04:13.446 --> 00:04:15.908
That's the way the human eye sees things.

48
00:04:17.169 --> 00:04:22.465
Cool! Let's move on to another famous painting of yours, The Last Supper.

49
00:04:23.746 --> 00:04:31.633
It's much larger than the Mona Lisa—8.8 meters wide and 1.6 meters high, painted on the wall of

50
00:04:31.673 --> 00:04:33.815
a church dining room in Milan.

51
00:04:34.876 --> 00:04:41.341
Unlike the Mona Lisa, it's actually a finished painting, but my work on it progressed very slowly.

52
00:04:42.422 --> 00:04:48.847
On some days I painted all day long without stopping, but on other days I just stared at

53
00:04:48.887 --> 00:04:51.465
the painting, thinking about how to proceed.

54
00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:56.528
I'm afraid the pace of my work greatly frustrated the church's prior!

55
00:04:57.428 --> 00:05:01.130
The finished painting shows Jesus' last meal with his disciples.

56
00:05:02.211 --> 00:05:07.393
I must tell you, Faye, I struggled with how to depict the faces of Jesus and his betrayer,

57
00:05:07.453 --> 00:05:13.076
Judas. On the advice of a friend, I left Jesus' face incomplete.

58
00:05:14.157 --> 00:05:18.979
As for Judas—I may have used that annoying prior as the model!

59
00:05:20.632 --> 00:05:27.074
From your paintings we can see that you were interested in things like light, perspective, and geometry.

60
00:05:27.994 --> 00:05:32.975
In fact, you kept notebooks filled with your thoughts, ideas, and plans.

61
00:05:33.996 --> 00:05:38.317
Your curiosity about all aspects of the world shines through.

62
00:05:38.337 --> 00:05:41.998
Was there anything you weren't interested in?

63
00:05:42.018 --> 00:05:45.779
No. I was curious about it all!

64
00:05:46.859 --> 00:05:55.583
I dissected human bodies at hospitals, recorded observations about nature, and imagined futuristic things like robots and flying

65
00:05:55.643 --> 00:06:00.084
machines. But I didn't just dream of fantastic inventions.

66
00:06:01.145 --> 00:06:05.327
I also tried to figure out how they could be made and how they might work.

67
00:06:06.547 --> 00:06:13.490
I'd love to read all of the brilliant entries in your notebooks, but your handwriting makes that um,

68
00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:15.251
a little difficult!

69
00:06:16.551 --> 00:06:21.989
Oh yes, I wrote backward, in mirrored handwriting, from right to left.

70
00:06:23.469 --> 00:06:29.491
I was left-handed, by the way, but exactly why I wrote in mirrored handwriting will remain my secret!

71
00:06:30.491 --> 00:06:34.513
Yet my notebooks might not be as interesting as you think, Faye.

72
00:06:35.473 --> 00:06:40.254
I wrote about ordinary things too, such as an inventory of my clothing.

73
00:06:41.315 --> 00:06:45.916
That's how we know you liked to wear colorful clothing—like pink tights.

74
00:06:47.596 --> 00:06:50.558
I really approve of that fashion choice, by the way!

75
00:06:54.181 --> 00:07:02.107
Getting back to your life Throughout your career you worked for many noble and rich families in Florence,

76
00:07:02.227 --> 00:07:03.808
Milan, and Rome.

77
00:07:04.609 --> 00:07:10.293
But in 1515, you left Italy for a special opportunity in France.

78
00:07:11.574 --> 00:07:17.597
I moved there to join the service of King Francis, who bestowed upon me the title of Premier

79
00:07:17.677 --> 00:07:20.858
Painter, Engineer, and Architect to the King.

80
00:07:21.878 --> 00:07:28.920
The king generously gave me my own castle, and he visited me frequently to discuss plans and ideas

81
00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:30.261
that he wanted me to work on.

82
00:07:31.321 --> 00:07:36.382
Sadly, I was an old man by then and had limited use of my right hand.

83
00:07:37.423 --> 00:07:41.304
I regretted not having created more art earlier in my lifetime.

84
00:07:42.352 --> 00:07:48.354
If it's any consolation, today your paintings are some of the most treasured masterpieces in the world.

85
00:07:49.175 --> 00:07:52.456
And you gave your genius to us with your notebooks.

86
00:07:53.437 --> 00:07:55.637
Thank you for sharing your life with us today.

87
00:07:56.598 --> 00:07:59.119
I'll let you get back to history now, Leonardo.

88
00:08:00.139 --> 00:08:01.580
It was my pleasure, Faye.

89
00:08:05.041 --> 00:08:11.864
Leonardo da Vinci's health deteriorated further while he was in France, leaving him bedridden.

90
00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:19.743
He died in 1519 at the age of 67 and was buried in a chapel within the royal

91
00:08:19.843 --> 00:08:28.366
castle. Leonardo accomplished so much during his lifetime that we were only able to talk about a small

92
00:08:28.486 --> 00:08:29.607
amount of it here.

93
00:08:30.567 --> 00:08:38.771
Today he is considered a genius for his beautiful artwork as well as his unique curiosity about subjects

94
00:08:38.851 --> 00:08:44.378
as widely ranging as military weapons, human anatomy, and architecture.

95
00:08:45.479 --> 00:08:50.382
Believe it or not, we're still learning new things about him and his works.

96
00:08:51.603 --> 00:08:52.984
I hope you enjoyed the show.

97
00:08:53.805 --> 00:08:58.408
I'll see you next time when we talk with another of history's greatest stars.
